.TH HDA 4 "2026-10-02" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
hda \- Intel High Definition Audio controller driver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B device hda
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B hda
driver supports audio controllers conforming to the
.I Intel High Definition Audio Specification
revision 1.0a \(em PCI class 0x04, subclass 0x03, the successor to
AC'97 \(em and the Intel functions firmware presents as class 0x04,
subclass 0x01 when the audio DSP is enabled (8086:9d71, 9dc8, 06c8,
a0c8, 51c8, 4dc8).  It attaches to any such controller found on the PCI bus and
registers the first codec with a usable analog output path as an
.BR audio (4)
device, published as
.B /dev/audio0
and
.BR /dev/dsp0 .
.PP
Playback only.  The driver programs one output stream descriptor; it
does not implement capture, so
.B AUDIO_GETPROPS
reports
.B AUDIO_PROP_PLAYBACK
alone.
.SS Controller
All registers live in a single MMIO BAR, which is moved below 4 GiB if
firmware placed it above (and the function brought to D0 if it was left
in a low-power state).  At attach the driver stops every DMA engine,
verifies each has stopped, resets the controller, waits the 521 microseconds the
specification requires for codec enumeration, and brings up the CORB and
RIRB command rings at whatever size the controller advertises in
.B CORBSIZE
and
.BR RIRBSIZE .
.PP
Output stream descriptors follow the input ones in the register block, so
the descriptor driven is index
.IR ISS ,
not zero.  Playback uses a cyclic ring of 32 buffer-descriptor entries of
4 KiB each, refilled from a 256 KiB software FIFO by the completion
interrupt.
.PP
The engine starts once eight buffers are staged; audio the application
stops adding to is padded out and started after 30 ms, or at once on
.BR SNDCTL_DSP_POST .
A stream that runs dry is stopped once a whole buffer of silence has
followed the last audio, so its tail is played out.
.PP
An interrupt is mandatory.  The ring is refilled from the completion
path, so a controller with no working interrupt is not attached rather
than being published as a device that would stop after its first few
buffers.  MSI is tried first, then the firmware's interrupt line, then
legacy INTx routed through the I/O APIC by the conventional PCI swizzle;
each is proven by provoking a codec response interrupt three times
before it is used.
.SS Codec
Every codec reported in
.B STATESTS
is asked for its vendor ID; one that does not answer is skipped, and the
rest are tried in turn until one yields an output path.  Within a codec
the driver powers the audio function group and every widget with its own
power control, locates the analog output converters and the output pins
that
.B CONFIG_DEFAULT
reports as physically connected, ranks the pins (speaker, then line out,
then headphone; association, then sequence), and selects the best pin
that some converter reaches through the codec's mixers and selectors.
.PP
Every pin in that pin's association, and every headphone pin, is then
driven too \(em through a second converter bound to the same stream if
it cannot reach the first.  There is no jack detection, so headphones
play alongside the speakers.
.PP
Digital widgets are skipped.  An HDMI or DisplayPort path additionally
needs digital converter control, channel mapping and ELD handling, none
of which is implemented, so such a pin would appear configured and stay
silent.
.PP
Amplifiers along the chosen paths are unmuted and set to their 0 dB step.
Amplifier parameters come from the widget only when it sets
.RB \(lq "Amp Param Override" \(rq;
otherwise the function group's defaults apply.  The external amplifier
enable (EAPD) is set on every pin that has one, since the amplifier is
often wired to a pin other than the speaker's.
.SS Formats
Linear PCM, 8/16/20/24/32 bits, 1 to 16 channels, at the rates the
selected converter advertises in its
.RB \(lq "Supported PCM Size, Rates" \(rq
parameter \(em a subset of 8, 11.025, 16, 22.05, 32, 44.1, 48, 88.2, 96,
176.4 and 192 kHz.  48 kHz is mandatory for all codecs and is always
accepted.
.PP
A rate, sample width or channel count the converter does not claim is
rejected with
.B EINVAL
rather than programmed and rendered at the wrong speed.  Changing the
format stops the stream and discards anything still queued, which was
recorded in the previous format.
.PP
Encoding is converted by the framework, not by this driver.
.BR AUDIO_ENCODING_ULAW ,
.BR AUDIO_ENCODING_ALAW ,
the unsigned encodings and
.B AUDIO_ENCODING_SLINEAR_BE
are translated to signed 16-bit little-endian on the way through, and the
driver is programmed for the converted format \(em so an 8-bit companded
stream runs the codec at 16 bits.
.BR AUDIO_GETINFO
continues to report the encoding the application selected.  See
.BR audio (4).
.SH DEVICE NODES
.TP
.B /dev/audio0 , /dev/audioctl0
Sun/NetBSD-compatible sample and control devices; see
.BR audio (4).
.TP
.B /dev/dsp0
OSS-compatible sample device.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B hda: \fIvendor\fB:\fIdevice\fB class \fIccsspp\fB oss=\fIn\fB iss=\fIn\fB codecs=\fImask\fB cad=\fIn\fB vid=\fIid\fB corb=\fIn\fB rirb=\fIn
Attach succeeded.  Reports the PCI IDs and class, the output and input stream
counts from
.BR GCAP ,
the codec presence mask, the codec address in use, its vendor ID, and the
negotiated command-ring sizes.
.TP
.B hda: codec \fIn\fB vid=\fIid\fB afg=\fInid\fB dac=\fInid\fB pin=\fInid\fB assoc=\fIn\fB dacs=\fIn\fB tag=\fIn\fB chan=\fIn\fB pcm=\fIcaps
The selected output path, its association, how many converters are bound
to the stream, and the primary converter's channel count and PCM
capability word.
.TP
.B hda: no codec detected
.B STATESTS
was empty after reset.  No codec is attached to the link, or the
controller never left reset.
.TP
.B hda: no analog output widgets (dacs=\fIn\fB pins=\fIn\fB)
The codec has no analog converter, or no output pin that
.B CONFIG_DEFAULT
reports as connected.  Common on display-audio-only controllers.
.TP
.B hda: no converter reaches an output pin
No connection path joins any output pin to a converter; the codec has no
output path.
.TP
.B hda: no usable interrupt; not attaching
No interrupt source passed its proof.  See
.B Controller
above.
.TP
.B hda: codec \fIn\fB did not answer; skipped
A codec present in
.B STATESTS
does not respond to commands (a display codec with its power well off,
for example).
.TP
.B hda: codec \fIn\fB stopped answering
Three commands in a row timed out; the codec is not asked again.
.TP
.B hda: \fIn\fB verb timeout(s) during configuration
The codec failed to answer within 50 ms.  The command rings are
resynchronised after each timeout, so a late answer cannot be taken for
the next command's; the graph was configured from incomplete
information and playback may be silent.
.TP
.B hda: \fIn\fB FIFO underrun(s), \fIn\fB descriptor error(s)
Reported on close.  Underruns mean the software FIFO was not refilled in
time; a descriptor error is fatal to the stream and forces a restart.
.TP
.B hda: \fIvendor\fB:\fIdevice\fB: snoop enable will not stick; not attaching
An ATI, AMD or NVIDIA controller refused the snoop-enable bit.  The
driver's DMA memory is cacheable, so the controller would fetch stale
ring contents.
.TP
.B hda: \fIvendor\fB:\fIdevice\fB does not snoop, and uncached DMA memory is not available; not attaching
An ATI or AMD graphics HDMI audio function.
.TP
.B hda: ALC\fIn\fB headset coefficient 0x46 = \fIvalue
The headset-jack configuration a Realtek ALC255-class codec came up
with, reported for diagnosis.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR audio (4),
.BR uac (4),
.BR audioctl (1)
.PP
.I High Definition Audio Specification, Revision 1.0a
(Intel Corporation, 17 June 2010).
.SH HISTORY
The
.B hda
driver first appeared in Substrate alongside the
.BR audio (4)
framework.  It was audited against the 1.0a specification and the
FreeBSD and NetBSD drivers in August 2026.
.SH BUGS
Capture is not implemented.
.PP
HDMI and DisplayPort output are not implemented; digital pins are
ignored during path selection.
.PP
There is no mixer control beyond the framework's gain and balance, and no
runtime selection between speaker and headphone pins \(em jack-presence
unsolicited responses are discarded rather than acted on, so both play.
.PP
The Realtek ALC255-class headset-jack coefficient is reported, not set.
.PP
DMA buffers are ordinary cacheable memory.  This is correct on Intel
controllers (whose no-snoop enable is cleared) and on any controller
whose PCIe snoop bit could be set; controllers that cannot snoop are not
attached.
